{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n19 \n \n \nitself because the flame is seen to be shifted toward the supply tube.  There was significant \nimprovement when the starting burner (serial number 1002) was replaced mid-way through \nround 1 with an alternate burner (serial number 1003), which continued to be used for spherical \nflame testing throughout the remainder of ACME operations.  Nonetheless, the symmetry was \nnot perfect, which is a shortcoming of the Flame Design data. \n \nOn 27 Dec. 2021 (GMT 361), it was determined that the Thin Filament Pyrometry (TFP) fiber array \nwas not aligned to the ACME Data Camera (DataCam) as intended, i.e., so that the fibers would \nbe orthogonal relative to the camera.  Unfortunately, this was the fourth day of testing in which \nthe fiber array wasn\u2019t aligned, where it regrettably wasn\u2019t recognized earlier.  Given the \nimportance of the TFP-derived measurements of the flame temperature, testing was halted until \nthe array was aligned (with other crew tasks) by astronaut Raja Chari on 17 Jan. 2022 (GMT 017).   \n \nThe signal from the standard ACME radiometer array used in round 1 of Flame Design testing was \nsaturated for nearly every test, yielding non-useful data. For round 2, that sensor array was \nreplaced with the one designed for Cool Flames Investigation with Gases (CFI-G) which yielded \nsatisfactory measurements. \n \nACME\u2019s Mass Flow Controllers (MFCs) used to control the flow of fuel, nitrogen, and oxidizer did \nnot always perform adequately, where the flow rates were not always appropriately steady or at \nthe intended value.  However, the resulting effects on the flame behavior are tentatively assumed \nto be minor. \n \nIn addition to sections 3.A-B, shortcomings are discussed in Appendices B and C of this report and \nin the ACME Engineering Implementation document.  The latter document includes discussion of \ndeviations and waivers (in section 4.B), etc. \n \n \n4. Preliminary Results  \nA. Changed Test Plans  \nA change was made to the CIR combustion chamber\u2019s pressure limit on 17 Jan. 2022 (by astronaut \nRaja Chari) to enable Flame Design testing at chamber pressures of up to 3 atm in the final round-\n2 test days, whereas the limit had been 1.5 atm in preceding testing.  In his request for this change, \nFlame Design PI Rich Axelbaum explained: \n\u201cOne of the fundamental goals of Flame Design is to achieve sooting limits.  We are unsure", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 19}}